# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Single assignments", item 2.
id: assign-minus-three
title: A subtraction that can go negative
topic: Assignment
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Single assignments" 2
tags: assignment, wp, counterexample

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the assignment for the
  postcondition $Q$. Then use the consequence rule to decide whether the
  triple is valid. If it is not valid, give values of the variables that
  show it.

pre: x >= 0
post: y >= 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  y := x - 3      // i1
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of y := x - 3
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression ($x - 3$) in the
  postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(y := x - 3, Q)
  = (y >= 0)[y <- x - 3]
  = x - 3 >= 0            // y is replaced by x - 3
  = x >= 3                // add 3 to both sides
set: R1 = x >= 3

step: The precondition must imply the weakest precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE
text:
  $R1$ is the weakest precondition of the program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R1$.
vc: x >= 0 ==> x >= 3
status: fails
counterexample: x = 0
why:
  The state $x = 0$ satisfies $P$ and does not satisfy $x >= 3$. From
  this state the program sets $y$ to $0 - 3 = -3$, so $y >= 0$ is false at
  the end, whatever the initial value of $y$.
result: The triple is not valid. The state $x = 0$ is a counterexample.

summary:
  $R1$ is exactly the set of initial states from which the assignment
  reaches $y >= 0$. The precondition $x >= 0$ also allows $x = 0$, $1$ and
  $2$, and each of them is a counterexample.
